WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.740
Is "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" going&nbsp;
to be ready by its December 2023 release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.520
date? Considering how many times it's&nbsp;
been reshot, we'd certainly hope so.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.720
With the cancellation of "Batgirl," an&nbsp;
uncertain future following the hiring of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.580
James Gunn and Peter Safran as co-heads of&nbsp;
DC Studios, and a yearlong string of bombs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.380
in the record books for 2023, there are only&nbsp;
two more films for fans to look forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.840
before Gunn leads a reboot of the universe with&nbsp;
"Superman: Legacy" in 2025. Aside from "Blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.760
Beetle," which premieres on August 18, there's&nbsp;
"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom." And right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.960
it's set to be a major Christmas season&nbsp;
tentpole for the studio on December 20.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.100
The production of "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom"&nbsp;
has been fraught with shakeups, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.260
ultimately resulted in three sets of reshoots.&nbsp;
From executive changes to differing expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.000
from DC's higher-ups to negative test screenings,&nbsp;
there have been a number of delays that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.540
resulted in the film's release being pushed back&nbsp;
from December 2022 to December 2023. But a fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.535
set of reshoots have the studio optimistic&nbsp;
that things might turn out well after all.

00:01:01.535 --> 00:01:02.760
"The tides are changing. Tides are changing!"

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.220
"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" has&nbsp;
been in the works for so long that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.720
it has managed to survive through three&nbsp;
regime changes at DC Studios. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.560
that means each set of execs wanted&nbsp;
different things from the property. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.220
sequel was greenlit by Toby Emmerich, former&nbsp;
head of the Warner Bros. film department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.180
while principal shooting was overseen by&nbsp;
Walter Hamada, who served as the head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.100
DC Films under Emmerich. Both men departed&nbsp;
as the film was undergoing test screenings.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.200
Those test screenings resulted in two sets&nbsp;
of reshoots greenlit by Emmerich and Hamada's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.480
replacements, Michael De Luca and Pamela Abdy,&nbsp;
as well as new Warner Bros. Discovery CEO David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.580
Zaslav. "The Hollywood Reporter" noted that Abdy,&nbsp;
in particular, was hands-on with the sequel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.040
oversaw a new cut. However, her version did even&nbsp;
worse with test audiences than the Hamada one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.480
When James Gunn and Peter Safran entered the&nbsp;
picture, a third set of reshoots were greenlit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.040
which spanned five days — and apparently&nbsp;
pleased all involved. Unsurprisingly, each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.540
set of executives wanted something unique from&nbsp;
the material that each previous cut didn't have.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.200
For instance, Hamada wanted to use&nbsp;
Michael Keaton's version of Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.100
Wayne not just in the second "Aquaman"&nbsp;
film, but across DC's slate of movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.460
as a sort of guide and leader for&nbsp;
the younger generation of heroes.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.240
"What's the play!? Batman, what do we do!?"
"We try not to die."

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.920
Unfortunately, the ever-shifting quicksand&nbsp;
of DC Films resulted in a skewed timeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.960
Under the Michael De Luca and Pamela Abdy regime,&nbsp;
reshoots involving Ben Affleck's take on Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.040
were filmed to make it more harmonious&nbsp;
with its place in the extended DC world.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.420
However, it appears that none of this material&nbsp;
will make it into the final version of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.140
as James Gunn and Peter Safran didn't&nbsp;
want to build a story promising the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.500
continuation of a universe that's&nbsp;
set to be officially abandoned.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.835
"This world must die."

00:02:45.835 --> 00:02:48.780
While that means a lot of the film's&nbsp;
original plot has been changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.320
at least those missing moments might&nbsp;
make for fun deleted scenes. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.700
you add these internal conflicts to the burden&nbsp;
already shouldered by overworked VFX houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.660
you end up with a movie that keeps getting&nbsp;
pushed back from its original debut date.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.740
"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom" has also&nbsp;
been shaped by repeated sets of test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.460
screenings. Several of these screenings&nbsp;
have resulted in audience displeasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.200
with story clarity being an ongoing concern.&nbsp;
This sent the show's producers and executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.160
back to the drawing board to bring the&nbsp;
best possible "Aquaman" sequel to life.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.560
The end result is several cuts of the film making&nbsp;
their way into the editing process. The magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.340
declared that the latest set of reshoots,&nbsp;
greenlit by James Gunn and Peter Safran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.380
went extremely well. Reportedly, the reshoots&nbsp;
only took four days instead of the five scheduled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.860
and seem to have finally gelled the film&nbsp;
into what Warner Bros. wants it to be.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.100
There's no word as to what's been&nbsp;
changed in the final cut of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.660
but at least this winter DC fans will finally&nbsp;
get to find out what all the fuss was about.

